```text
01
EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
The Cheyenne Municipal Courts are currently in a temporary location while the City Council and City of
Cheyenne consider the purchase of 308 West l7” Street. The City Council has asked the City of Cheyenne
to further investigate the possibility of utilizing this site for the permanent location of the Municipal Courts
within the Project Budget of $9 million. The existing building on this site has been vacant for 15± years with
minimal upkeep, therefore the existing condition of the building presents concerns regarding safety and
potential impacts on the Project Budget. Noel Griffith, Jr. & Associates Architects, the project architect for
the Municipal Courts Projects has assembled a Design Team to review the information related to this
investigation and present the findings in a Feasibility Study.
Information pertaining to the existing condition of the site was obtained by the City and provided to the
Design Team for review. This information included a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment, an Asbestos
Containing Material Survey and Work Plan, as well as a Topographic and Boundary Survey.
The Phase
I
Environmental Site Assessment concluded that a Phase II Environmental Site Assessment is not warranted,
and the most significant ernhronmentai risk is the confirmed presence of asbestos containing material. The
Asbestos Containing Material Survey confirmed and located the asbestos containing materials and this
information was used to establish a budget for abatement. The Topographic and Boundary Survey provided
the information needed to confirm that this site meets the initial program requirements as established for
the Site Proposal Location Evaluation.
The NSA Design Team conducted a Structural Assessment of the existing building and determined the
shoring requirements for asbestos abatement as well as investigated the structural connection of the
structure immediately adjacent to the west. The findings from this report indicate that abatement can be
accomplished by utilizing the structural shoring methods outlined in the report and the building on this site
can be demolished without compromising the structural integrity of the adjacent building.
The Design Team compiled and reviewed the information provided by the City and the information obtained
in this investigation to present in this Feasibility Study. Every attempt to account for typical construction
obstacles and challenges was made to establish a responsible Project Cost Model. The Project Cost Model,
at this early stage of the Project, is based on industry standard pricing and preliminary assumptions,
including the project consisting of a one-story building without a basement. The findings validate that the
$9 million Project Budget is adequate to complete the Municipal Court Project at 308 West 17th Street, as
the cost analysis indicates that the preliminary cost model reflects a Project cost of eight million, eight
hundred sixty-one thousand dollars ($8,861,000).
