02
INTRODUCTION
The Cheyenne Municipal Courts (Courts) are currently located in the City Municipal Building at 2101 O’Neil
Avenue, a temporary location due to the Courts vacating the County Building. The City of Cheyenne (City) is
considering the purchase of 308 West 17th Street (Site) to serve as the permanent location for the Courts.
The City Council (Council) wishes to confirm the costs associated with the demolition of the existing structure
on the Site to validate the preliminary demolition budget of $1.2 million prior to authorizing the purchase
of the property. Council’s end goal is to ensure that the existing structure can be demolished, and a single-
story Municipal Court building can be built at this location within the $9 million Project Budget.
To accomplish the goal of Council, to confirm that this project can be completed within budget, the City has
requested professional services to review the information it has received thus far and to complete the cost
analysis of the Courts Project Budget for Council’s review.
Noel Griffith, Jr. & Associates Architects (NGA)
has assembled a team of professionals (Design Team) to accomplish this task and present the findings in this
Feasibility Study.
The Design Team also worked closely with Trihydro Corporation and Terracon
Consultants, Inc. who were hired directly by the City.
The Design Team consists of the following professionals:
•
Noel Griffith, Jr. & Associates Architects (NGA), Architect.
•
KL&A Structural Engineers and Builders (KL&A), Structural Engineer.
a
S&S Builders LLC (5&5), General Contractor (GC), cost consultant.
•
Asbestos &Technical Services LLC (ATS), General Abatement Contractor (GAC), cost consultant.
2.1
ARCHITECTURAL SUMMARY
This Site has been frequently referred to as the Fowler Building, Zs Furniture, and the Carey Building. The
address has varied over the years and has included 300-308 West 171)1 Street. It is located on the corner of
Carey Avenue and 17th Street in Cheyenne, Wyoming. It appears the original building was built in 1876 with
several additions and remodels since, with historically retail space on the main level and basement with
apartments located in the upper levels.
The building underwent a major renovation in the 1950’s that
included removing several of the interiorwalls on the main level, as well as the addition of the exterior metal
façade and steel awnings. The property has been vacant for 15± years with minimal upkeep. There have
been several active roof leaks, broken windows, and water pipe leaks that have led to concerns with the bird
infestation and extensive mold growth, as well as water damage to finishes and structural elements. The
building has asbestos containing materials on both the interior and exterior of the building.
The existing building on this Site is primarily a two-story building with a portion of the building extending to
3 stories.
This building has a partial basement under a majority of the original building footprint.
The
building footprint is approximately 132 feet (along Carey Avenue) and 109 feet (along l7 Street), for a total
of approximately 14,500sf. The Site itself is 0.33 acres in size and is relatively flat with a gentle slope to the
southeast corner of the lot. The Site is not located in the FEMA flood zone. The Site is listed as a non-
contributing structure on the State or Federal Historic Registry.
The current use of adjoining properties to the Site: North
- Alley and Nelson Museum of the West; East,
Carey Avenue and Provisions Wine & Spirits; South
- West 17th Street and Pioneer Avenue Parking Garage
and Cheyenne Transit Program; West - Collectors Club, Knights of Pythias, and Bohemian Metals.
Refer to Appendix I for site and existing building photos for reference.
