03
STRUCTURAL ASSESSMENT
Due to the age and condition of the existing building on the Site and the potential impact on project budget,
the structural condition of the building was investigated. The information provided in the Trihydro Phase 1
ESA (Appendix A) regarding surface soils and groundwater conditions are reviewed below as well as the
Structural Assessment for Abatement Activity (SA) completed by KL&A, Inc. (Appendix H)
3.1
2018 TRIHYDRO PHASE
I ESA
2018 Trihydro Phase 1 ESA, Section 2.3, discusses surface soils, which do not pose a structural concern for
this Site.
It is important to note that soils testing will need to be completed to provide the needed
information for a proper structural design.
We recommend this to be completed after demolition is
complete and the site has been backfilled with structural fill, as designed by a geotechnical engineer. This
is typical for any construction project of this size.
2018 Trihydro Phase 1 ESA, Section 2.4 states that the groundwater at the Site is unknown, however the
anticipated depth to groundwater, based on nearby monitoring wells, is approximately 11 to 15 feet below
ground surface.
Since a basement is not proposed for this project, it is anticipated that no specialized
structural considerations will be needed for groundwater issues.
3.2
KL&A STRUCTURAL ASSESSMENT
KL&A’s Structural Assessment (SA) focused on observing the existing conditions of the building and
identifying areas of structural concern regarding workersafety during the abatement process and identifying
locations where temporary shoring of the structure is recommended.
KL&A also assessed the structural
attachment of the Carey Building to the adjacent building to the west and the potential impacts to the
demolition.
3.2.a
Areas of Structural Concern for Abatement Activities
Over the years, the building has had extensive water damage from apparent leaky roofs and water pipe leaks
in basement. The majority of the damage observed was to the ceiling, floor, and wall finishes. However,
there are areas within the building that the structural roof and floor decking, roof rafters and floor joists
have visibly degraded enough to be of concern for abatement activity. Included in this Section is a summary
of the SA recommendations in conjunction with the areas of abatement activities that are affected by these
structural concerns for reference.
Structural Decking Shoring: There are several areas of potential hazards of localized structural decking decay
on the roof as well as an area on the 2 floor.
It is recommended that any abatement activities on areas
that appear to have decay or degradation to the roof/floor decking are performed from ‘A” structural
plywood decking laid on top of, and perpendicular tO, existing solid joist framing. The decking does not need
to be physically attached to the existing floorjroof structure and therefore may be moved and reused in
other work areas. It is recommended that any abatement activities on the roof utilize this method.
JoistiRafter Shoring: The areas indicated in the SA to be of structural concern with the condition of the
existing joists and rafters where abatement activities are required will need to be supported by temporary
structural framing for the duration of the abatement mitigation activity in the area. The temporary framing
may consist of 2x stud wall framing or wood(steel beam framing supported by series of wood or steel
columns placed beneath the suspect joists. Blocking between existing joists to prevent rolling of the joist
shall be installed. These shoring lines shall be added at each level below the specified level down to the
6
