06
BUDGET ANALYSIS
Every attempt to foresee certain obstacles and challenges were made to establish a responsible Project Cost
Model, however it is important to note that this is a cost model and not a guaranteed project cost. There
are several variables that the City
I Design Team I Contractor will have control over and there are other costs
associated with this Project that are established linfluenced by the industry.
The Project Cost Model
presented in this Report is based on preliminary assumptions as discussed throughout this Report and
summarized in this Section.
The Project Cost Model will be updated jadjusted after each design phase:
Project Programming, Conceptual Design, and the Design Development. Each iteration of the Project Cost
Model will allow the City and Design Team to evaluate the Project Program and align with the established
Project Budget.
To establish a strong foundation for the Project Cost Model, NSA has included two cost consultants to
contribute their expertise to establish and confirm the budgetary items included in the Project Cost Model:
Jerry Butcher, S&S Builders LLC, General Contractor (SC)
— Cheyenne, Wyoming
David Blackwell, Asbestos & Technical Services LLC, General Abatement Contractor (SAC)
— Cheyenne,
Wyoming
6.1
PROJECT BUDGET ASSUMPTIONS
6.1.a
Time Line Assumption
It is assumed that the design and construction of the Court’s Project is desired to be completed as soon as
possible. To establish the Project Budget it will be assumed that the Design Team will receive a notice-to-
proceed by August 2018 and construction will commence in 2019
To account for an extended time table, an increase of 4% per year will need to be considered for
inflation I escalation costs if the Project’s commencement extends beyond the time line assumption above.
6.1.b
Historic Element Assumption
It is assumed that this project will not pursue a historic rehabilitation or historic replica in the design or
construction of the Court’s building on this Site.
There are varying degrees of historic rehabilitation, restoration, and replication. If it is desired to investigate
options related to the historic element of this Site, a more extensive architectural and structural evaluation
will be needed to ascertain what additional structural elements or techniques would be needed to
accomplish this and if this existing structure will meet the program needs of the Courts. To provide a cost
magnitude for a historic rehabilitation, restoration, or replication, and its impact on the Project Budget,
consider increasing the Project Budget by $500,000 for adding minima) historic elements and as much as
$15 million for a full historic restoration or rehabilitation, neither of which is currently considered in the
Project Cost Model.
6.1.c
Program Assumptions
To establish budget parameters, the following assumptions apply:
•
on-site parking will be required for staff, public will utilize the Spiker Parking Structure
•
single level (above grade) structure, slab-on-grade with no basement or crawl space
•
industry standard exterior design elements and construction materials
•
the Courts will be the only tenant in the building
not seeking LEED accreditation
